intro = The game consists of 108 cards:
    .item1 = One "game leader" card that changes hands each turn.
    .item2 = 5 "Activity" cards representing the activities that can be performed during each turn.
    .item3 = 4 "Earnings" cards indicating the gains made during a software deployment.
    .item4 = 31 "Product Team" cards
    .item5 = 54 "Agile Maturity" cards
    .item6 = 13 "Agile Value" cards

print = Print the cards

leader-section = Game Leader
    .description =
    The game leader is the player who decides the first activity performed in a game turn.
            The other players will, one after another, decide on the following activities.
            At the end of the turn, the card changes hands to give everyone the opportunity to decide the first activity of a turn.

            When the card changes hands, only 6 potential cards are kept by the players.
            Indeed, as time goes by, potential diminishes. This is inevitable in any organization: ideas that have not been brought to fruition wither, lose their momentum, and only a few manage to endure.

activities-section = Activities
    .description =
    A software development team performs many activities. We focus on five that are of interest here.

            Meetings between peers at public events or internal sessions within an organization are essential for discovering and deepening good practices. For many agile practitioners, a 'Conference' is the starting point of the journey.

            The 'Development' activity goes far beyond simply writing code: it is an opportunity for exchanges between technical experts and business experts, and to ensure the quality of deliverables with tests as an integral part of the process.

            A product only has value once it is in the hands of users. Deploying frequently facilitates this user feedback and, long considered secondary, 'Deployment' is regaining a central place.

            A method is not agile without continuous adaptation. During the 'Retrospective', a team has the opportunity to analyze its situation and improve it.

            In case of a specific need, the intervention of a 'Coach' provides an external perspective and facilitates the team's skill development.

teams-section = Teams
    .description =
    Product teams are the heart of software creation. Organized by value streams, they are each responsible for a specific type of software.

            In our video game company, four specialties coexist: Advergames, Educational Games, Community Games, Massively Multiplayer Online Games.

conference-maturity-section = Conference
    .description =
    How to maximize the benefits of peer encounters? Start by participating actively: share your skills and experiences.

            The organizers, true facilitators, maintain these communities and, thanks to their many contacts, enrich and disseminate best practices.

development-maturity-section = Development
    .description =
    Development activities gain in quality thanks to many practices.

            Pair Programming establishes a permanent dialogue between developers and facilitates skill sharing.

            By prioritizing explicit names, modularity, and tests, Clean Code ensures readable and scalable code, which accelerates value production.

deployment-maturity-section = Deployment
    .description =
    Continuous Delivery consists of having software that is always ready to be delivered, which minimizes the effort required for a delivery and allows for more frequent deliveries.

            Each delivery is a learning opportunity. Potential product improvements increase by giving a large place to user experience and even more by involving these users in the product design.

deployment-earnings-section = Earnings
    .description =
    Earnings fluctuate but some areas are always more lucrative than others.

retrospective-maturity-section = Retrospective
    .description =
    The retrospective is a regular meeting where the team reviews its functioning and identifies concrete areas for improvement for each iteration.

            The Passionate Developer intervenes by sharing their technical know-how and enthusiasm, which accelerates skill acquisition and reduces the time spent training new arrivals.

            Structured Feedback Sessions encourage the free expression of feedback on deliverables and processes, thus offering additional opportunities to immediately adjust practices.

            The DevOps practice harmonizes development and operations through automation and continuous integration, allowing initiatives underway to be valued twice during deployment to reinforce operational flexibility.

            The Internal Coach supports the team on a daily basis with proven methods, reducing the effort required for each improvement action and smoothing collective progress.

long-term-goals-section = Long Term
    .description =
    Long-term maturity is based on consolidated practices that sustainably structure the organization.
            Agile HR optimizes talent and skills management, sustainably increasing the company's potential while strengthening the team's decision-making and operational capacity.
            Software Craftsmanship, through technical excellence and attention to code detail, reduces friction and accelerates feature delivery.
            The Agile Sensei supports teams in their continuous learning, promoting collective autonomy and skill development.
            A clear Product Vision aligns stakeholders and directs efforts towards the real needs of users.

smells-section = Smells
    .description =
    Smells refer to practices whose application creates concrete inefficiencies.

            Detailed Planning immobilizes teams in a vision that is too rigid: the effort invested in ultra-precise estimates often proves to be obsolete, penalizing responsiveness.

            The use of a separate Test Team introduces feedback delays and unnecessary back-and-forth, slowing down the continuous guarantee of quality.

            An Application Framework offers a ready-to-use solution, attractive for accelerating deliveries, but becomes a hindrance when expertise grows and teams wish to explore other directions.

            Agile Certifications rarely guarantee cross-functional skills: often aligned with a specific method, they provide little indication of overall mastery of practices.

values-section = Values
    .description =
    Adherence to a set of values is the foundation of any agile approach.
            Values reflect a preference: preferring collaboration over silos, simplicity over complexity, rapid feedback over cumbersome documentation.
            Scrum highlights five essential values: commitment, focus, openness, respect, and courage.
            Extreme Programming also emphasizes communication, simplicity, feedback, courage, and respect.
            When these fundamental choices are shared and understood, their impact does not just add up, it multiplies, propelling the organization towards exponential potential.